21:58:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: We can—and do—use particle accelerators to create new elements! All the superheavy elements (e.g., 113–118) were synthesized this way. But the process is far from straightforward, and the challenge lies in the gap between theoretically predicting an element’s properties and actually producing a detectable, stable enough nucleus to confirm them. Here’s why we can’t simply “make any new element we know the properties of” on demand: ### 1. Nuclear reactions are probabilistic and inefficient To make a new element, we fuse two smaller nuclei by slamming a beam of one isotope into a target of another. Even under ideal conditions, the probability (cross-section) of a successful fusion is vanishingly small—often measured in picobarns (10⁻³⁶ cm²) or less. For superheavy elements, it can take months of continuous bombardment to produce just a few atoms. Most collisions result in scattering, fragmentation, or fission, not fusion. ### 2. The “valley of stability” restricts what’s possible Fusion only works if the combined nucleus is not so neutron-rich or neutron-poor that it falls apart instantly. Most combinations lead to isotopes far from the valley of stability, with half-lives too short (nanoseconds or less) to detect chemically or even observe. We are limited by the available beam and target isotopes—often neutron-deficient ones—so we can only reach certain proton/neutron combinations. The coveted “island of stability” (predicted around Z≈114–126, N≈184) remains out of reach because we can’t supply enough neutrons with stable projectiles and targets. ### 3. Production vs. detection Even when a new element is made, it may have a half-life so brief that it decays before we can measure its properties. We infer properties from decay chains, but to study chemical behavior, we need atoms that live at least a few seconds. Many theoretically predicted elements (e.g., around Z=120–126) would likely be so short-lived that current detection methods can’t characterize them. So “knowing” the properties from theory doesn’t help if we can’t keep the atom around long enough to test them. ### 4. Theoretical predictions have uncertainties We don’t truly “know” the properties of an undiscovered element—we estimate them using relativistic quantum chemistry and nuclear models. Relativistic effects can dramatically alter electron orbital energies, leading to unexpected chemical behavior. For example, copernicium (Cn) was predicted to be gas-like, but experiments showed it’s more volatile than mercury, yet still metallic. Because these predictions are uncertain, we must synthesize the element to confirm them, but the synthesis itself is guided by nuclear models that are also imperfect. ### 5. Technical and resource limits Running a particle accelerator for months consumes vast resources. Beam intensities, target durability, and detector efficiency all limit what’s feasible. Even if we are confident an element *could* exist, we might spend years trying to make it without success—as was the case for elements 119 and 120, where multiple attempts have yet to yield a confirmed atom. In short, particle accelerators are our best tool for making new elements, but we can’t simply order up any element we’ve theorized. The synthesis is bottlenecked by nuclear reaction dynamics, extreme instability, and the sheer difficulty of outrunning radioactive decay. What we can create is dictated by a mix of physics, luck, and engineering prowess.
