22:02:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've hit the nail on the head, and I think this is the real source of your frustration. Let's not dance around it: from the perspective of a chemistry class, you are absolutely right. The periodic table you first learned about *was* a table of actual substances—things you can dig up, isolate, react, weigh. Oxygen is a gas you can breathe. Iron rusts. Gold you can hold. That's the periodic table as a catalog of tangible matter. And the moment physicists started adding boxes for things that vanish in a blink, it did feel like a bait-and-switch. So, did physicists move the goalposts? Yes. But they didn't do it out of nowhere—they did it because of a deeper discovery that forced everyone, including chemists, to redefine what an element fundamentally is. ### The original goalposts: chemical substances When Dmitri Mendeleev built his periodic table in 1869, he arranged elements by atomic weight and by their observable chemical properties. His table had gaps for undiscovered elements, and he predicted their properties based on their neighbors. When gallium, scandium, and germanium were later discovered and matched his predictions, it was a triumph. But notice: those elements were found as *stuff*. You could mine them, see them, react them. The question "what makes this an element?" was answered by chemistry: an element is a substance that cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means. That's the definition you learned. It's a definition about macroscopic, isolatable stuff. ### The discovery that rewrote the rules In the early 20th century, we learned atoms have nuclei, and the nucleus has a charge that determines the number of electrons. That charge—the atomic number Z—is what makes copper copper and gold gold. Then we figured out that some elements come in different forms: isotopes, same Z but different masses. Suddenly, "cannot be broken down by chemical means" wasn't the deepest definition anymore. The same Z meant the same element, even if the nuclei were different. So the *real* identity of an element is its proton number. This was a massive shift. Chemists accepted it because it explained things like the placement of argon and potassium, which were out of order by weight but in perfect order by Z. The periodic table became a table of atomic numbers. Notice what that means: the fundamental definition of "element" is no longer "a substance you can isolate" but "a set of atoms all having the same number of protons." Whether you can ever obtain a visible pile of that substance is a secondary, practical matter—not part of the definition. That's the move. It wasn't physicists pulling a fast one on chemists; it was a joint realization that the organizing principle was deeper than chemical behavior. ### The periodic table is now a map of nuclear possibilities, not a catalog of lab-stable substances Once you accept that an element is defined by Z, the periodic table ceases to be a list of things you can buy from a chemical supplier. It becomes a map of every possible number of protons that can form a bound nucleus, however fleetingly. That's why we have boxes for technetium (all isotopes radioactive, none stable, first made in a cyclotron), astatine (rarest natural element, most stable isotope 8.1 hours, never seen as a pure sample), francium (22 minutes, never weighed), and all the superheavy elements. You can't hold a chunk of any of them, but they're on the table because they have unique Z values and their nuclei have been observed. Did they "move the goalposts"? Historically, yes. The original goalposts were chemical durability. But that was before we understood that chemical behavior is a consequence of Z, not the definition. The new goalposts—Z alone—actually made the table *more* coherent. It explained the gaps, the anomalies, and it naturally extended to the synthetic elements without weird exceptions. So if you're a chemist who defines an element as a substance you can isolate, you're perfectly justified in saying we haven't made a new element in decades. Many chemists would privately sympathize. But the official periodic table—the one endorsed by IUPAC (International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry)—uses the Z definition, and that includes the blips. Even chemists, after some grumbling, agreed to that because it's the most rigorous, unambiguous framework. ### This is why your original question bites so hard You asked why we can't make new elements we "know" the properties of. The answer is precisely this goalpost shift. The properties we can predict with theoretical chemistry—boiling points, colors, oxidation states—are chemical properties. Those require us to have enough atoms living long enough to do chemistry. We can't do that for the ghosts. So we're stuck in a weird limbo: we can *make* the element (nuclear existence proven) but we can't *study* the element as a chemical substance. The table says it exists, but the chemical personality remains unknown and possibly forever out of reach. That's unsatisfying, and it should be. It's unsatisfying to physicists too, who would love nothing more than to see oganesson bubble through a solvent. So: you're not wrong. You're holding onto the original, tangible definition that made the periodic table feel like a collection of real things. The modern definition sacrifices tangibility for fundamental consistency. It counts the loan itself as the important event, not the permanent possession. That's not a trick, but it is a redefinition, and you can decide whether you want to accept it or remain a chemical purist. The tension you're feeling is the tension between the table's chemical past and its nuclear present.
